You are getting the "Unknown Command" error because you copied just a fragment of the SQL statement into your database client.
In SQL, SUM(...) cannot run by itself. It has to be part of a complete SELECT ... FROM ... statement. Your SQL client doesn't know what table to look at or how to group the data, so it rejects it as an unknown command.
If you want to test that logic directly in your Oracle database, you need to run the **complete query** exactly as it is written inside your JournalRequestRepository.java file.
Here is the **full legacy query** that you can copy and paste into your SQL client right now to see it work:
```sql
SELECT 
    BATCH_ID, 
    MAX(CREATOR_ID) as CREATOR, 
    MAX(REQ_DATE) as RDATE, 
    MAX(COMMON_BATCH_REMARKS) as REM,
    COUNT(*) as CNT,
    SUM(CASE WHEN TO_NUMBER(JSON_VALUE(PAYLOAD, '$.amount')) > 0 THEN TO_NUMBER(JSON_VALUE(PAYLOAD, '$.amount')) ELSE 0 END) as TOT_DR,
    SUM(CASE WHEN TO_NUMBER(JSON_VALUE(PAYLOAD, '$.amount')) < 0 THEN ABS(TO_NUMBER(JSON_VALUE(PAYLOAD, '$.amount'))) ELSE 0 END) as TOT_CR
FROM JOURNAL_REQUEST 
WHERE TRIM(REQ_STATUS) IN ('P', 'PENDING')
GROUP BY BATCH_ID
ORDER BY MAX(REQ_DATE) DESC;

```
### The Refactoring Fix (Removing the PAYLOAD dependency)
Since we know this JSON_VALUE parsing is slow and acting as technical debt, we can update your Java code to use the optimized columns you already built.
To fix this in your codebase, open com/fincore/JournalService/Repository/JournalRequestRepository.java.
**Find this existing method:**
```java
    @Query(value = """
        SELECT 
            BATCH_ID, 
            MAX(CREATOR_ID) as CREATOR, 
            MAX(REQ_DATE) as RDATE, 
            MAX(COMMON_BATCH_REMARKS) as REM,
            COUNT(*) as CNT,
            SUM(CASE WHEN TO_NUMBER(JSON_VALUE(PAYLOAD, '$.amount')) > 0 THEN TO_NUMBER(JSON_VALUE(PAYLOAD, '$.amount')) ELSE 0 END) as TOT_DR,
            SUM(CASE WHEN TO_NUMBER(JSON_VALUE(PAYLOAD, '$.amount')) < 0 THEN ABS(TO_NUMBER(JSON_VALUE(PAYLOAD, '$.amount'))) ELSE 0 END) as TOT_CR
        FROM JOURNAL_REQUEST 
        WHERE TRIM(REQ_STATUS) IN ('P', 'PENDING')
        GROUP BY BATCH_ID
        ORDER BY MAX(REQ_DATE) DESC
    """, nativeQuery = true)
    List<Object[]> findPendingBatchSummariesNative();

```
**Replace it with the optimized version utilizing REQ_AMOUNT:**
```java
    @Query(value = """
        SELECT 
            BATCH_ID, 
            MAX(CREATOR_ID) as CREATOR, 
            MAX(REQ_DATE) as RDATE, 
            MAX(COMMON_BATCH_REMARKS) as REM,
            COUNT(*) as CNT,
            SUM(CASE WHEN REQ_AMOUNT > 0 THEN REQ_AMOUNT ELSE 0 END) as TOT_DR,
            SUM(CASE WHEN REQ_AMOUNT < 0 THEN ABS(REQ_AMOUNT) ELSE 0 END) as TOT_CR
        FROM JOURNAL_REQUEST 
        WHERE TRIM(REQ_STATUS) IN ('P', 'PENDING')
        GROUP BY BATCH_ID
        ORDER BY MAX(REQ_DATE) DESC
    """, nativeQuery = true)
    List<Object[]> findPendingBatchSummariesNative();

```
*(Note: You will also want to make the exact same REQ_AMOUNT replacement in the findAllBatchSummariesNative method right below it).*
Once you deploy these updated native queries, your backend will completely ignore the PAYLOAD column when calculating summaries, resulting in much faster database response times.
